      "commentary": "Now the teaching proper begins, and it begins with a paradox designed to short-circuit the grasping mind: the One does not move, yet it is faster than thought. Read that slowly. Whatever is swiftest in you — the mind that can leap to the moon and back in an instant — is still too slow to catch this, because it has already arrived. Pūrvam arṣat: it ran on ahead. The senses, the devas, sprint after it and never close the gap.\n\nWhy can't the fastest thing in us reach it? Because we are trying to catch what is doing the catching. The eye cannot see itself; the flashlight cannot shine on its own source of light; awareness cannot turn around and grasp the awareness by which it grasps. Every time the mind lunges to seize the Self, it finds only another thought — because the seeker is the sought. The verse is not describing a fast-moving object. It is describing the still center from which all movement is seen, the one thing always already present in every experience, which is exactly why it can never be reached as an experience.\n\ntiṣṭhat — standing still — tad dhāvato'nyān atyeti — it outruns the runners. This is the Upanishad's first great reversal, and it will echo through everything after: the deepest reality is not found by going faster or reaching further. It is found by stopping. The thing you are chasing is the one doing the chasing. And the wind — mātariśvā, \"that which grows in the mother\" — weaves the breath of every living being within this stillness. Even your next inhale is happening inside the very thing you were looking for."
