      "commentary": "The paradoxes come faster now, stacked deliberately so the mind cannot rest on any one of them. It moves and it moves not. It is far and it is near. It is within everything and outside everything. Each pair breaks a handle the intellect reaches for. The moment you conclude \"ah, it is transcendent, beyond and far away,\" the verse answers: near — nearer than near. The moment you settle into \"it is intimate, within me,\" it answers: outside all of this too.\n\nThis is not mystical fog; it is precision. Anything you can say about the Self, its opposite is equally true, because the Self is not a thing among things that could have one location or one property. It moves — because everything that moves, moves in it and as it. It moves not — because it is the stillness against which all motion is measured. It is far — because no amount of seeking travels the distance. It is near — because it is the very awareness reading these words, closer to you than your own thoughts.\n\nThere is a practical mercy hidden in this dizziness. As long as you believe the truth is far, you postpone it: later, after the retreat, once you have meditated enough. As long as you believe it is only near and private, you shrink it to a personal feeling. The verse refuses both escapes. What you are looking for is simultaneously the ground under everything and the intimacy of this very moment — which means there is nowhere to go to find it, and nothing to do but stop overlooking it."
