      "commentary": "After the metaphysics, the ethics — and the Upanishad shows its hand about why any of this matters. The one who sees all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings arrives at a single, very human result: na vijugupsate. The word is worth sitting with. It means to shrink from, to feel disgust, to recoil, to hide. When the vision lands, all of that stops.\n\nConsider where revulsion and contempt actually come from. We recoil from what we experience as utterly other — the stranger, the enemy, the version of a person we have decided is beneath us, and, quietly, the parts of ourselves we cannot bear to look at. Every act of othering rests on a felt conviction of separateness: that is not me, that has nothing to do with me. The verse locates the root of cruelty not in bad values but in bad seeing. Contempt is a perception problem.\n\nAnd so is its cure. To see the same Self looking out through every pair of eyes is not to force yourself to be nice; it is to lose the ground on which disgust stands. You cannot hold in contempt what you recognize as yourself. Notice, too, that vijugupsate includes hiding — the vision heals not only how you see others but the exhausting work of concealing yourself, because there is no longer an alien crowd to perform for. This is the quiet revolution the Upanishads propose: not a new commandment to love, but a way of seeing from which the failure to love simply falls away."
