      "commentary": "Verse six described the seeing; verse seven names its fruit, and the fruit is freedom from the two things that run every unawakened life: moha and śoka — delusion and sorrow. Where, the verse asks, could they even stand, once all beings have become the Self for one who truly sees? It is a rhetorical question with a precise answer: nowhere. They have lost their foothold.\n\nLook at what delusion and sorrow are made of. Delusion — moha — is fundamentally a confusion about boundaries: mistaking the passing for the permanent, the role for the self, the wave for something separate from the ocean. Sorrow — śoka — is the grief of separation: I have lost what was mine, I am cut off from what I love, I am alone against a world that is not me. Both are diseases of the boundary line. Both assume a small, walled-off self surrounded by others and by loss.\n\nDissolve the wall — ekatvam anupaśyataḥ, seeing the oneness — and you have not answered grief with an argument; you have removed the soil it grows in. This is subtle, and easy to caricature. The verse is not promising that the realized never weep, nor commanding you to suppress feeling. It is describing what happens at the root when the fundamental sense of being a separate fragment gives way. The tears may still come; the terror underneath them — that I am a lonely thing that can be finally severed from all it loves — is what dissolves. Beyond outcomes, beyond gain and loss, there is a way of standing in the world where sorrow has quietly run out of room."
