      "commentary": "The arc that began with the paradoxes of verse four culminates here, in a portrait — though \"portrait\" is the wrong word for something the verse insists is bodiless. Sa paryagāt: that One has gone everywhere, reached everything, pervaded all. And then a cascade of attributes, most of them negations: bodiless, without wound, without sinews, unpierced by evil. The Upanishad describes the ultimate the only honest way it can — by stripping away everything it is not. It has no body to be hurt, no seam to come apart, no wound through which corruption could enter.\n\nThen the positives, and they are staggering when read as a set: kaviḥ — seer, poet, the one who perceives; manīṣī — the thinker, the intelligent; paribhūḥ — the one who surrounds and transcends everything; svayambhūḥ — self-existent, arising from itself, dependent on nothing. This is not a distant deity on a throne. It is the intelligence and awareness at the root of things, needing no cause outside itself, encompassing every cause within it.\n\nThe verse closes with a line that quietly answers a question the anxious mind keeps asking: yāthātathyataḥ arthān vyadadhāt — it has apportioned all things exactly as they truly are, through endless years. Not carelessly, not cruelly, but yāthātathyataḥ — \"according to how things actually are,\" in perfect accord with truth. To the part of us that suspects the universe is a chaos we must frantically control, the Isha offers a different intuition: that reality is not a mistake to be corrected but an order to be understood. The rest of the text turns from what the Self is to how a human being, caught between knowledge and ignorance, life and death, is to live inside that order."
